Jove wrote:
>   Use pets and monsters.  Only step where they step.
>
>   Follow your own footsteps wherever possible.
>
>   A domestic pet (dog/cat) on a leash will whine when it's adjacent to
> both you and a trap.  Other pets may do this, I don't know for sure.
>
>   Keep an eye out for unusual monsters that may indicate a polymorph
> trap on a level.
>
>   Combine those with some patient searching.

In short: obsess over every step you take to the extent that the game
becomes unplayable, and probably starve in the end from constant
searching.

Or, recognize that that armor that can be destroyed by a polytrap (that
is, non-DSM) is probably armor that you won't be keeping for the long
haul anyway.

Also note that random polymorph traps are not generated when level
difficulty < 8 (level difficulty being the average of player and
dungeon levels).  Since traps are generated only when the player first
enters a level, and player experience usually goes up more than down,
this is another reason to take a quick trip downstairs and back up
whenever they're discovered on a level, at least in the early game.

